(June 26, 1916 – October 3, 2005) was an American football player.","title":"Alvin Wistert"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A native of Chicago, Illinois, he played college football at the tackle position for Boston University in 1946 and at the University of Michigan from 1947 to 1949.","title":"Alvin Wistert"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He began his collegiate football career at age 30 following 12 years of working in a factory and serving in the United States Marine Corps during World War II.","title":"Alvin Wistert"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He played at the defensive tackle position for the undefeated 1947 and 1948 Michigan Wolverines football teams, both of which finished the season ranked No. 1 in the final Associated Press poll.","title":"Alvin Wistert"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He also holds the distinction of being the oldest college football player ever selected as a College Football All-American, having been selected to the 1948 College Football All-America Team at age 32 and the 1949 Team at age 33.","title":"Alvin Wistert"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: He is the only player ever to score 1,000 points with two different teams.
