{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Prophet's Prey is a 2015 American documentary film directed by Amy J. Berg.","title":"Prophet's Prey"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film is an adaptation of the 2011 book "Prophet's Prey: My Seven-Year Investigation into Warren Jeffs and the Fundamentalist Church of Latter-Day Saints".","title":"Prophet's Prey"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was produced by Katherine LeBlond and Sam Brower, the author of the book, for Showtime and Imagine Entertainment.","title":"Prophet's Prey"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It premiered at the 2015 Sundance Film Festival and had its television premiere on October 10, 2015.","title":"Prophet's Prey"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The film's subject matter is Warren Jeffs, the president of the Fundamentalist Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints, who is now running his religion from the confines of the Texas state prison, where he is serving out a sentence of life plus twenty years for the rape of girls aged 12 and 13.","title":"Prophet's Prey"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The score is written by Nick Cave and Warren Ellis.","title":"Prophet's Prey"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Melanie Hogan (born 8 July 1977) is an award winning film director and producer, known for her works in Australian documentary cinema.","title":"Melanie Hogan"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Hogan became first known with her directorial debut "Kanyini" which premiered at the Sydney Film Festival in 2006.","title":"Melanie Hogan"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film came out of Hogan’s personal realization that she had not learnt anything about the history of her country, Australia, from an Aboriginal perspective despite studying in Australian Institutions right through to tertiary level.","title":"Melanie Hogan"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" She also lamented the fact that she did not know anything about the world's oldest living culture.","title":"Melanie Hogan"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 2008 Detroit Lions season was the 79th season for the franchise in the National Football League.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Lions entered their third season under head coach Rod Marinelli and were looking to improve on the 7–9 record they put together in 2007.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Instead, the Lions had one of the worst seasons in pro sports history.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The team lost all sixteen of their games in 2008, becoming the first team since the 1976 Tampa Bay Buccaneers to end an NFL season with no wins and no tied games as well as the first and only team to do it since the schedule was expanded to sixteen games.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Lions were mathematically eliminated from the playoffs by Week 11, when they stood 0–10.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The team's victory over the Kansas City Chiefs in Week 16 of 2007 stood as their last until Week 3 of the 2009 season.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" From the time the Lions recorded a win over the Denver Broncos in 2007 to reach 6–2, the team went 5–47 over their following 52 games (including all of 2008 and 2009) before winning four games in a row at the end of the 2010 season.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" As of 2017, this is the only team in NFL history to finish a season with a 0–16 record.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The 2016 Cleveland Browns and the 2009 St. Louis Rams came close to 0–16, both going 1–15.","title":"2008 Detroit Lions season"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Eye Of The Leopard, is a 2006 nature documentary film by National Geographic Channel that shows the journey, life, and growth of a young leopard cub named "Legadema".","title":"Eye of the Leopard"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film is narrated by Jeremy Irons, who also voiced Scar in Disney's 1994 animated film "The Lion King".","title":"Eye of the Leopard"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Irons would later narrate "The Last Lions", a 2011 National Geographic documentary film.","title":"Eye of the Leopard"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Last Lions is a 2011 African nature documentary film by National Geographic Society, videotaped and directed by Dereck and Beverly Joubert.","title":"The Last Lions"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was shot at the Okavango Delta in Botswana.","title":"The Last Lions"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film premiered at the Palm Springs International Film Festival in January 2011 and was released in select theaters on February 18, 2011.","title":"The Last Lions"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The film follows in the tradition of other National Geographic big cat films, such as "India: Land of the Tiger" and "Eye of the Leopard".","title":"The Last Lions"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Muhammad: Legacy of the Prophet is a PBS documentary film about the life of the Islamic prophet Muhammad based on historical records and on the stories of living American Muslims who call Muhammad the Messenger of God.","title":"Muhammad: Legacy of a Prophet"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was produced in 2002 by Alex Kronemer and Michael Wolfe of Unity Productions Foundation and Kikim Media.","title":"Muhammad: Legacy of a Prophet"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: The Last Lions
